### The parent chooses shows they can feel comfortable letting the child watch.

4 of 97 posts.

Parents of two- and three-year-olds ask for or recommend children's shows, with concerns about stimulation, attention, educational value and how watching feels for the adult. One simply asks for available shows, while another reports a successful alternative to Ms. Rachel. One parent promotes their own published show-ranking website and asks for feedback; those comparisons are that speaker's claims.

- "My son is 2. He loves Ms. Rachel, but I needed something different. We tried a few different new shows, but he didn't like them until we found Ms. Catherine/Jiggle Jam. I don't know why her channel doesn't have more followers! I find her stuff to be very interactive but less stimulating than Ms. Rachel. I like that she has more content like construction and space songs. Thought I'd share because she's great!" [ar068-0002]
- "I was looking for new TV shows for my kids (3 and 5 years old) and couldn’t find anything that actually felt relaxing to watch, or that I wouldn't have too much second thoughts about. Every recommendation online seemed to be just someone’s subjective taste. A few classic shows have some research behind them, but newer shows, YouTube channels, they’re just out there, unstudied." [ar059-0009]
- "It presents a ranking of 45 modern and classic children's shows, comparing them in terms of educational value, sensory load, scene cuts/min, words/min, sound intensity, visual intensity, and a few other criteria. All metrics are based as much as possible on objective measurements or published research." [ar059-0012]
- "Please give me shows for an active 2 year old that I can put on kids YouTube or Amazon prime, thank you." [ar053-0083]
- "I remember a thread a while back about YT channels that are the absolute worst - quick cuts that lead to decreased attention span and tantrums when taken away. My little girl (\~2.5 yo) is finally starting to become interested in videos. Mainly songs from Ms. Rachel. What else is really good for little kids on YT?" [ar073-0138]

**But:** The person publishing the ranking acknowledges limits to the method. "I'm aware that it's not an academically robust study (would need to spend countless more hours refining and validating the method) and probably not up to scientific research standards," [ar059-0014]

