### The parent weighs which bike the child is ready to ride.

4 of 97 posts.

These parents consider a first balance bike, pedals or a powered balance bike for children from 27 months to nearly three. They weigh demonstrated riding ability against age, fit, expense and how quickly the child may outgrow the purchase. One parent also asks how providing the next bike fits with teaching effort and limits.

- "How did you know when it was time to move up from your toddler balance bike to the next stage with pedals? We have a WOOM go and our 27 month old has been asking for pedals, we go on 3 mile rides about three times a week and he’s proficient at riding at this point. 27 months feels really young to transition to pedals . Looking for others experiences" [ar020-0058]
- "I only know what to do when it comes to adult bikes, and my first baby was a little princess, does anyone have experience with toddlers balance bikes? I've been looking at a few options online that caught my eye but i don't really know what to prioritise for his age. The Weight? Tire type? Seat height adjustability?" [ar077-0072]
- "My concern with a true balance bike is that she’ll also outgrow it very quickly, so it’s not worth the money." [ar020-0072]
- "My concern with pedals is that it will be too hard for a 2.5/3yo to learn to ride, since she’s never ridden a true balance bike. But she’s physically quite coordinated and confident, and picks up gross motor things pretty well." [ar020-0073]
- "Iv worked really hard to get to where I am today and am in a really good financial position un like my single mother was all those years ago. I can easily go and buy him whatever he wants. Id love to see him on that new bike ripping around and I want to be riding moto with him as soon as possible! How can I somehow teach him that he cant have everything and nothing comes easy? I feel he needs to achieve somthing. Like when he climbs a playground and yells "I did it daddy" he has drive and determination that I never want to take from him" [ar016-0030]

**But:** The age-based hesitation is accompanied by reports of the child's actual competence and desire to progress. "We have a WOOM go and our 27 month old has been asking for pedals, we go on 3 mile rides about three times a week and he’s proficient at riding at this point." [ar020-0058]

