# baby-development: pattern 4, What should I get for this age? (children of 2 and 3 years)

**Written:** 2026-11-20 from baby-development/evidence/2026-11/age-reread/digest-06.md
**Reviewed by the owner:** not yet

## Limits

A random, keyword-free sample of 20,000 posts from twelve parenting subreddits, 1 April–24 October 2026, yielded 3,811 on-niche posts, including 1,340 about children of two and three. This pattern has 97 on-niche and 27 near posts; each count is posts tagged by one reader, not parents or market prevalence. Forms across digest readers are merged by judgement; this single digest’s forms remain separate, including its infant and unknown-age material.

## On-niche posts

### Parents want toys that keep being used.

10 of 97 posts.

They weigh lasting interest, open-ended play, compatibility and size.

- "I'd rather have fewer things that actually stick around in his play." [ar015-0090]
- "He’s a big boy and is wearing 4t clothes at 2 right now." [ar038-0096]

**But:** Having toys does not secure interest: "She’s MUCH more interested in what I’m doing." [ar044-0040]

### Parents want activities they can use now.

7 of 97 posts.

They seek independent play, less mess, prepared activities and travel occupations.

- "I work FT and have a first grader as well, and I have zero creativity to create the activities myself." [ar007-0053]
- "Sketchpens and sheets of paper work great." [ar061-0001]

**But:** Activities may need adapting: "Some of the activities are above her head but we make them fun anyway." [ar003-0114]

### Parents and gift-givers want presents the child will use.

7 of 97 posts.

Occasions include birthdays and new siblings; gifts can also express a wish for closeness.

- "What's a good book/ coloring book/ small gift idea for a girl that's turning 3?" [ar063-0113]
- "I feel kinda bad and want to get her something cool that she won’t outgrow for a while." [ar077-0034]

**But:** A gift request also reveals difficulty playing together: "I also really hate playing kid stuff. We do goof off occasionally tho." [ar077-0037]

### Parents want devices that fit learning and communication needs.

7 of 97 posts.

Requests include apps without ads, learning pads and accessible audio or family communication.

- "I don't mind paying a little up front but I really don't want ads or purchases while she's using it." [ar079-0052]
- "We are considering a phone for my son so he can utilize a talk to text app and still communicate with his papa." [ar066-0132]

**But:** Screen limits meet immediate needs: "We want to reduce screen time but, with the arrival of a new baby we really need activities for her and we are thinking to put her iPad to use (which have been accumulating dust in a corner drawer)." [ar043-0137]

### Parents want a bed setup that is safe and practical.

7 of 97 posts.

They weigh falls, independent access, airflow, shared rooms and room for an adult.

- "My wife likes floor beds to prevent her rolling and falling off." [ar050-0061]
- "I'd rather have it a few inches up on slats." [ar050-0062]

**But:** Rails also complicate bedding changes: "I personally think she is at no risk of falling out, but I am nervous that the one night I choose to remove it, she will fall." [ar063-0073]

### Parents seek stories that match growing understanding.

6 of 97 posts.

Books address differences, siblings, interests and the move toward chapter books.

- "we want to teach her that this is "normal" that we can be friends with everyone and always be nice and respectful." [ar009-0001]
- "At what age did you start moving from picture books to chapter books?" [ar080-0054]

**But:** Topic alone does not ensure fit: "I’ve found some other books online but some are more geared towards older kids." [ar039-0019]

### Parents follow the interests their child already shows.

5 of 97 posts.

Music, tools, maps, dragons and jewelry prompt specific requests, sometimes for shared play.

- "Then I thought, maybe I should get him is first toolbox, but with actual tools, not toy versions." [ar044-0037]
- "Are there any board games or activities y’all know of that would let him interact with this idea more without being on a screen?" [ar083-0092]

**But:** Buying a version of the parent’s belongings may disappoint: "Do they like it as much as they seem to love ours or is it a waste of money?" [ar051-0097]

### Parents want equipment for movement and shared outings.

5 of 97 posts.

They consider bikes, water play and climbing or jumping equipment within space limits.

- "The main thing is that we’re really together, not each of us staring at our own screens in the back seat." [ar074-0040]
- "Our daughter is 2.5 and LOVES to climb and jump." [ar009-0041]

**But:** nothing found against it.

### Parents need transport equipment that fits the child and outing.

5 of 97 posts.

Fit, compactness, public transit and flights matter; this form also includes infants.

- "We have Joolz’s old Hub+ model that works fine but her feet dangle horribly, nowhere to rest them." [ar025-0026]
- "She doesn’t fit into her current carrier anymore." [ar025-0018]

**But:** Equipment may be unnecessary: "At home, we rarely use her stroller at this point. If we go for walks she mostly doesn’t want to sit in it and just walks." [ar048-0114]

### Parents hesitate to give up a crib that supports good sleep.

5 of 97 posts.

Room moves, potty training, a coming baby or height force reconsideration.

- "He seems to be content with crib life." [ar061-0050]
- "I posted this because after two years, our youngest was finally sleeping almost perfectly through the night….and now the toddler bed is massively disrupting the peace and quiet we finally achieved lol." [ar038-0127]

**But:** Some want the change: "The reason we would like to switch to a toddler bed is to give her more independence in the morning." [ar070-0073]

### Parents need feeding equipment that fits changing bodies and habits.

5 of 97 posts.

Chairs and cups must accommodate size, chewing and household constraints; one child is five months.

- "They’ve got one now that they have in their toddler bed, but they’ve chewed a hole in the straw." [ar042-0062]
- "He still needs to be in a high chair with a tray to keep him away from our small dog while he is eating." [ar051-0065]

**But:** Seat space is not always the problem: "It’s getting him in and out, and not being able to get him out quickly makes me nervous." [ar039-0012]

### Parents want footwear that fits active feet and the weather.

5 of 97 posts.

They seek room to move, comfort, warmth and appropriate materials.

- "Looking for something not so heavy and chunky, but waterproof and warm!" [ar034-0123]
- "We've been doing the target and Walmart generic velcro shoes, but I just don't feel like they ever look very comfortable." [ar073-0136]

**But:** Preferences vary by setting: "Our son is always barefoot at home, but we are thinking towards either having him keep his Crocs or switch to grippy socks; I'm not sure why, but being barefoot at daycare is giving me the ick 😄" [ar007-0097]

### Parents seek shows they feel comfortable letting children watch.

4 of 97 posts.

They ask about stimulation, education and adult enjoyment; one promotes their own ranking website.

- "Every recommendation online seemed to be just someone’s subjective taste." [ar059-0009]
- "I find her stuff to be very interactive but less stimulating than Ms. Rachel." [ar068-0002]

**But:** One parent simply asks what is available: "Please give me shows for an active 2 year old that I can put on kids YouTube or Amazon prime, thank you." [ar053-0083]

### Parents weigh riding ability against age and the next bike’s cost.

4 of 97 posts.

They compare balance bikes, pedals and powered options, including how to teach effort.

- "My concern with a true balance bike is that she’ll also outgrow it very quickly, so it’s not worth the money." [ar020-0072]
- "My concern with pedals is that it will be too hard for a 2.5/3yo to learn to ride, since she’s never ridden a true balance bike." [ar020-0073]

**But:** Actual skill can exceed age expectations: "We have a WOOM go and our 27 month old has been asking for pedals, we go on 3 mile rides about three times a week and he’s proficient at riding at this point." [ar020-0058]

### Parents want flotation gear for enjoyable pool play.

4 of 97 posts.

Size limits and concern about learning to swim shape their choices.

- "I’m thinking he’s too young for those water wings that go over your arms?" [ar054-0026]
- "She’s too small for the puddle jumper." [ar048-0036]

**But:** They limit the intended use: "And again, it’s just for the resort pools, not planning to use it all the time or in open water." [ar009-0106]

### Parents seek products that make difficult care routines easier.

4 of 97 posts.

Requests cover hair washing, toothpaste, dentist familiarization and thumb guards.

- "We've tried so many different brands and flavours but she hates them all." [ar022-0067]
- "I'm worried that my toddler won't be able to climb things or have trouble doing other tasks." [ar042-0109]

**But:** The toothbrush request is for familiarization: "Dentist recommended something simple like the Quip because we’re not buying it out of necessity." [ar030-0069]

### Parents want practical guidance for a new family stage.

3 of 97 posts.

They compare communication books and seek explanations, routines and sibling preparation.

- "I’ve got a 2.5 year old and another on the way, and honestly it feels like everything is fragmented." [ar035-0017]
- "Looking for a book/resource I can read about introducing newborn siblings that aligns with Montessori principles." [ar083-0073]

**But:** nothing found against it.

### Parents need potty-training items that suit this child.

2 of 97 posts.

Character underwear may motivate; potty dimensions may obstruct success.

- "My dtr JUST started potty training and now he wants underwear too to incentivize him to potty train." [ar022-0039]
- "Because of his size, he consistently pees over the toilet seat because there's not a pee shield high enough to block his penis properly." [ar055-0104]

**But:** The existing seat partly works: "We have one of those things that go over a regular toilet with little stairs, which he's pooped in successfully a few times." [ar055-0103]

### A parent wants a secure harness for a child who runs off.

1 of 97 posts.

A city and amusement-park trip makes containment urgent despite already having AirTags.

- "The wrist leashes won’t hold him for a second so I need a harness for sure." [ar039-0124]
- "He runs away at parks when he’s “ready to go home”, so it’s a total safety issue to have him completely free in a busy place." [ar039-0123]

**But:** The parent also says: "He’s a great kid. Listens for the most part, very smart, no disabilities that we are aware of, he’s just so active." [ar039-0125]

### A parent wants an accessible room that stays organized.

1 of 97 posts.

They ask how to arrange clothes, toys, books and outgrown belongings.

- "Books: Where do you keep them, and do you have them accessible to your toddler?" [ar062-0115]
- "Outgrown clothes: Do you have a system for separating clothes that no longer fit from the stuff currently being worn?" [ar062-0117]

**But:** nothing found against it.

## Near posts

### Parents hesitate to leave a crib that preserves sleep.

4 of 27 posts.

Children’s preferences and friends’ reactions accompany worries about escaping and containment.

- "We offer her the bed every night but she still chooses the crib every time." [ar039-0055]
- "We have it so good why mess it up? Anyone still have their 3 year old in the crib?" [ar056-0121]

**But:** Climbing can force action: "The dreaded day has come, my 2 year old (25 months) just launched himself out of his crib (luckily I was there and caught him)." [ar004-0109]

### Some speakers promote or propose digital development resources.

4 of 27 posts.

These are promotions or feedback requests, with children’s ages unspecified, rather than purchases.

- "I just love cartoon nursery rhymes of this youtube channel "JoJo Jelly Rhymes"." [ar023-0041]
- "What would make an app like this valuable enough for you to use regularly? At what point would you consider paying for it?" [ar049-0055]

**But:** Not all seek payment: "I've made the web app completely free to use." [ar030-0125]

### Parents want bedroom safeguards that withstand growing abilities.

3 of 27 posts.

They anticipate climbing, wandering and access to stored belongings.

- "They, thankfully, are unaware they are fully capable of climbing out of the crib." [ar042-0027]
- "At what age do you fully trust they will not wander around the house and hurt themselves?" [ar031-0041]

**But:** Curtain risk is disputed: "My husband isn’t convinced by vague threats and I don’t want to have unnecessary anxiety." [ar042-0028]

### Parents seek calmer apps and safer device content.

3 of 27 posts.

One post solicits opinions on a school learning platform; ages are not always given.

- "Ive seen a few but they tend to be too chaotic and overstimulating with their visuals/noise/colors." [ar010-0120]
- "We set up the Amazon Kids+ app on our daughter's tablet and it's been solid in terms of no ads, no chat features, just books and a few learning games." [ar077-0054]

**But:** Default recommendations failed one parent: "One mistake I made was trusting the default app store recommendations, which led to some sketchy stuff slipping through." [ar077-0054]

### Parents seek books beyond overwhelming or incomplete recommendations.

3 of 27 posts.

Requests cover summer reading and puberty; a retrospective account recommends a monthly book program.

- "Does anyone know of books or resources online that can help me teach my daughter about puberty?" [ar049-0053]
- "My daughter already finished two graphic novels in the LGBTQ+ genre but she likes all genres or at least willing to try new ones! Any suggestions are welcome!" [ar038-0069]

**But:** The program’s reported benefit involved being read to: "Our kid mostly enjoyed being read to vs. reading on their own, but they were definitely ahead of the curve on literacy when they got into school because of how much we exposed them to books at those formative years." [ar019-0085]

### Parents weigh travel equipment against fit, comfort and cost.

3 of 27 posts.

They consider plane seating, a wagon and anticipated infant transport.

- "I'm looking for a very lightweight, collapsible stroller wagon for travel." [ar043-0132]
- "She’s also almost fully potty trained so I know getting in and out of it on the plane is potentially a tantrum waiting to happen." [ar062-0066]

**But:** An expectant parent questions extra equipment: "I've seen strollers that have a lie-flat bassinet in them but surely people aren't buying extra strollers just for 6 months or less?" [ar064-0103]

### Parents choose sleep clothing around warmth and independence.

2 of 27 posts.

A toddler’s sleep sack also inhibits climbing; an infant cannot adjust blankets.

- "My son is 2 years and 2 months and is still in a sleep sack." [ar077-0041]
- "She can't do that yet, so I have to nail it at the outset!" [ar009-0052]

**But:** Awake ability does not settle the question: "Is there a general guide? My worry is, if we transition out of them right now, though he CAN pull a blanket on himself, he may not do so in his sleep and then he'll be cold and wake up (and then I'll have to wake up lol)." [ar077-0042]

### A parent needs small-waisted underwear for potty training.

1 of 27 posts.

Readiness arrives while the child still wears smaller infant sizes.

- "She’s just over 2 and is still wearing 9-12 month denim shorts and 12-18 month cycling shorts." [ar020-0069]
- "Primark leggings usually fit her well but do their pants also fit small around the waist?" [ar020-0069]

**But:** nothing found against it.

### A parent wants evidence before choosing a pillow for development.

1 of 27 posts.

Their own kyphosis motivates concern about spinal alignment.

- "We noticed last week he has been putting his blanket under his head sometimes, and considered getting a pillow." [ar044-0001]
- "I personally suffered kyphosis when younger (still do but have "controlled" it with exercise) and want to give him every chance to not endure what i did when younger." [ar044-0001]

**But:** The claimed benefit lacks proof in their search: "I couldn't find any type of actual proof of this outside the chiro world, but was wondering what yall's take was." [ar044-0001]

### An expectant parent compares play gyms for changing sensory needs.

1 of 27 posts.

The baby’s stage is unknown; varied sensory toys and reversible sides appeal.

- "Hi, FTM here due in August 😊 I’m looking to get a play gym and was considering the Fisher Price kick and play." [ar066-0086]
- "Haven’t seen it mentioned much - does anyone have play gyms or toys from them and can share your thoughts/experience? Thanks!" [ar066-0087]

**But:** nothing found against it.

### A parent wants to lighten a child’s independent school journey.

1 of 27 posts.

The middle-school cyclist must carry school supplies without lockers; age is unspecified.

- "He has to carry his chrome book, pe clothes, lunch, water plus books and notebooks." [ar050-0084]
- "Looking for ways any experienced moms have helped cut some weight." [ar050-0085]

**But:** The child rejects an option: "He won’t go for a basket (not cool, mom!)." [ar050-0085]

### A parent wants additional containment for beach outings.

1 of 27 posts.

They describe a possibly autistic two-and-a-half-year-old who runs off and plan pool practice.

- "I'm also thinking I'll feel better about taking him into the water (and not far at that) if we have a tether of some kind." [ar070-0049]
- "I'm planning on taking him to swim in pools in advance of this, I found [this](https://youtu.be/veQ4Bg4veRw?si=AYKqgG8_ZPZHWBL1) a helpful primer on how to teach him basic swimming." [ar070-0048]

**But:** The existing leash may not transfer: "I have a backpack and leash for him that I use in crowded situations where he can't be running off...but I don't think that would work in the water with a life vest." [ar070-0049]

## Across the on-niche posts

### Parents have already tried activities, products and routine changes.

78 of 97 posts.

Attempts include toys, screens, room preparation, potty training and care routines.

- "I’ve tried everything I can think of to make this an easier experience for him but nothing I’ve tried has worked." [ar012-0067]
- "We have gotten into the habit of having the TV on way more than we’d like to." [ar007-0052]

**But:** Adaptation can work: "Some of the activities are above her head but we make them fun anyway." [ar003-0114]

### Other people shape parents’ choices through advice, offers and rules.

24 of 97 posts.

Dentists, neighbors, peers, partners, manufacturers and daycare all appear.

- "I've been told by our pediatric dentist to buy the anti-thumb sucker contraption made of silicone to stop the habit." [ar042-0107]
- "My wife seems to think the bunk bed is a good idea too. But she is not strongly pushing anything." [ar063-0091]

**But:** Recommendations leave questions: "I’ve heard great things about baby bogs, but are they actually warm enough?" [ar034-0123]

### Speakers report buying toys, gifts and equipment, sometimes with disappointment.

7 of 97 posts.

The bought tag includes replacements, an old highchair purchase and a parent’s own childhood-to-adult history.

- "So far I bought 3 outfits and a blanket but im willing to spend around $50 more." [ar082-0002]
- "This is already a replacement and I’d rather find something that they won’t chew through in a week." [ar042-0063]

**But:** The tag is not seven recent child purchases: "I bought my first dirtbike as soon as I could afford one at the age of 20." [ar016-0026]

### Parents describe guilt, exhaustion and worry while making choices.

23 of 97 posts.

They also express anticipation and pleasure in shared play.

- "It was a gift and I’m feeling guilty that it’s not working for us right now 🥲" [ar039-0010]
- "My son is 2.5 and we’ll be transitioning soon but I am overwhelmed. Someone please help!" [ar056-0130]

**But:** Feelings also include joy: "It’s literally been the joy of my life to play and use our imaginations together." [ar047-0081]

